Use `Export records` to save the active project's records as a CSV, TSV, Excel,
or JSON file. A saved export preset decides which records become rows, which
columns they have, and what the columns are called.

## Quick way to export records

{% steps %}

1. Open the project menu and select `Export records`.
2. Choose an `Export preset` {% ref id="A" /%}. To create or change one, select
   `Manage presets` {% ref id="B" /%}.
3. Check the preset summary: its record type, header format, and columns.
   Select `Preview Table` {% ref id="D" /%} to see real rows. On a phone, the
   preview is under the `Preview` tab {% ref id="F" /%}.
4. Choose the file format {% ref id="C" /%} and type a file name. Turn on
   `Append date` {% ref id="G" /%} to add the date to the name. JSON cannot use
   Darwin Core headers.
5. Choose where to save the file and select `Export records` {% ref id="E" /%}.
6. Open the file and check the rows, headers, units, and repeated values. Use
   `Share` to send it, or `Export another` to make another file.

{% /steps %}

## Deep dive

A tabular export preset is a saved recipe for one table. It decides three
things:

- **Which records** become rows: the `Record type`, and for specimens the
  `Specimen taxon group`.
- **Which columns** each row has, set under `Edit Fields`.
- **What the headers are called**, set by `Generated header format` or by
  naming each column yourself.

You change these in `Settings` > `Tabular Data`, not on the export screen. Field
and setting changes save as you make them. The preset's name and its optional
`Description` (up to 80 characters, shown under the name in the list) save only
when you select `Update`. Use `Duplicate` to start a new preset from an existing
one.

### What one row is

| Record type | One row per |
|---|---|
| `Specimen` | Specimen. Its parts and other repeated details can be joined into one cell or spread across numbered columns. |
| `Specimen parts` | Specimen part, such as a skin or a tissue tube, with its specimen's details repeated on each row. Suits tissue inventories. |
| `Site` | Site. |
| `Collecting event` | Collecting event. |
| `Narrative` | Narrative entry. |
| `Symbionts` | Symbiont record. |

### Kinds of columns

Each column in `Selected Mappings` is one of four kinds, chosen as its
`Mapping type`:

| Mapping type | Use it for | Example output |
|---|---|---|
| `Single field` | One NAHPU field, as stored or reformatted. | `Rattus` |
| `List field` | Several fields joined, skipping empty ones. | `Indonesia, West Java, Cibodas` |
| `Custom field` | Fields mixed with your own text, or text alone. | `HH-156` |
| `Nested records` | Repeated records, such as a specimen's parts or collectors. | `Skin; Skull; Tissue` |

Check a field under `Insert Source Field` (`Available Fields` on a phone) to
add it as a `Single field`. Use `Add custom field` and `Add nested` for the
other kinds. Select a column's pencil to change it, and drag its handle to
reorder it. Custom fields and conditional output are explained in
[Export expressions](/en/usages/export/export-expressions/#custom-fields).

### Format a single field

A `Single field` column can reformat its value with `Value format`:

| Value format | What it does |
|---|---|
| `Normal text` | Writes the value as stored. |
| `Encoded text` | Writes the label of a stored code, such as `Male` for `0`. |
| `Coordinates` | Writes a coordinate in the format set under `Advanced`. |
| `Date` | Writes a date in the format set under `Advanced` > `Raw format option`, such as `dd/mm/yyyy`. |
| `Conditional brackets`, `Conditional field`, `Conditional value` | Bracket or replace the value when conditions match; see [Conditional output](/en/usages/export/export-expressions/#conditional-output). |

Every kind of column can also use `Replace text` to rewrite values after
formatting, for example to rename a part type for the receiving database.

### Repeated values

Some values repeat: a specimen can have many parts, collectors, or collecting
methods. `List field` and `Nested records` columns choose how to write them:

| Output | Result |
|---|---|
| `One column` | All values in one cell, joined by the separator you choose. |
| `Indexed columns` | One column, or one group of columns, per value, such as `prep_1_type`, `prep_2_type`, … The number of columns follows the record with the most values. |
| `Repeated rows` | Nested records only: one row per value, with the rest of the record repeated. Only one column per preset can do this. |

With `Indexed columns` or `Repeated rows`, each child field of a nested column
can have its own `Column name (optional)`. Write `{n}` where the number goes:
`PrepType{n}Count` writes `PrepType1Count`, `PrepType2Count`, and so on. A name
without `{n}` takes the number in the `Indexed column names` style.

Preview a record with several values to check that the shape suits the
receiving software.

### Column headers

`Generated header format` names every column you have not named yourself:

| Format | Header for `specimen::uuid` |
|---|---|
| `table::fieldName` | `specimen::uuid` |
| `fieldName` | `uuid` |
| `Darwin Core (dwc:/dcterms:)` | `dwc:occurrenceID` |
| `NAHPU namespace (nahpu:table.field)` | `nahpu:specimen.uuid` |

A name typed into a column's `Column name` always wins. Choose the format the
recipient asks for, then check the headers in the preview.

With Darwin Core headers, a field that has no Darwin Core term keeps a NAHPU
header; the format does not turn every observation into a standard term. Darwin
Core output also:

- credits the identification to the specimen's determiner;
- keeps verbatim coordinates;
- adds a positive specimen coordinate extent to a positive coordinate
  uncertainty. Check that combined value against the project's
  [georeferencing method](/en/best-practices/data-collection/#georeferencing).

Measurement units are not converted; check them in the output.

### Blank columns

A receiving sheet may have columns NAHPU does not record, such as a continent.
Add a custom field, give it a column name, and leave its value empty. It is
written as an empty column, so the file still lines up with the sheet.

### Additional observation fields

Custom fields you defined in `Settings`, such as environmental observations,
export like any other field. Their source keys use the field's ID, such as
`customEnvironment::<definition UUID>`. Event and specimen exports include
stored values even when those fields are hidden in the form; hiding a field
does not filter it out of exports.

### File formats

| Format | Use it for |
|---|---|
| CSV or TSV | Exchange with databases and scripts. |
| Excel | Reviewing in a spreadsheet. |
| JSON | Software that reads JSON. Darwin Core headers are not available. |

Open the file in the tool that will use it. Spreadsheets can drop the leading
zeros of IDs and reinterpret dates.

The [LSUMZ Mammalogy tutorial](/en/tutorials/export/specify-export-preset/) builds a
complete preset for a museum database, using most of the options on this page.

## Transfers and recovery

A record export is a one-way copy: NAHPU cannot import it back as a project.
To move or restore data in NAHPU, use:

- a [project transfer](/en/usages/export/project-transfer/) or a
  [NAHPU Data Package](/en/usages/export/export-bundles/) to move a project to
  another installation, with `Import project` on the home screen, or to combine
  contributions with `Merge project` in an existing project;
- a [database backup](/en/usages/export/#backup-database) to restore a whole
  installation.

To share one preset with your team, see
[Create a tabular export preset](/en/how-to-recipes/export-and-print/create-export-preset/).
